SM(539,{"n":"1 Corinthians 9 Part 2","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/1 Corinthians 9 Part 2.mp3","d":2685.3,"s":[[0.16,"Come and study Holy Scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.08,"Father Stephen is a former Protestant pastor and Bible teacher in the Reformed Church, but now an Orthodox priest, serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[21.28,"So chapter 9, verse 10, or does he say it altogether for our sakes?"],[25.68,"For our sakes, no doubt, this is written, that he who ploughs should plough in hope, and he who threshes in hope should be partaker of his hope."],[34.72,"If we have sown spiritual things for you, is it a great thing if we reap your material things?"],[40.24,"If others are partakers of this right over you, are we not even more?"],[44.82,"So he just sort of makes the application specific."],[49.43,"Uh sounds almost like he's arguing that they should start doing this."],[54.23,"Well, he is coming for a visit, but no, that's not his point."],[57.35,"That's not his point."],[58.39,"Um, but if if anybody asks uh why priests get paid by churches, this is why."],[66.59,"Uh-huh. This is why."],[68.6,"And strictly speaking, this and this is important for properly understanding the priesthood."],[76.04,"Uh priests being a priest is not a job."],[80.86,"And that's not the way St."],[83.66,"Paul talks about it here."],[85.6,"Because you notice the jobs he points to as examples have to do with farming and things."],[90.88,"It's not jobs like where you have an employer and you receive wages."],[96.58,"That's not the example."],[98.68,"The examples he uses."],[100.36,"Uh priests don't. have a job"],[104.0,"The church supports them so that they don't have to have a job."],[110.0,"And so they could devote their time to the spiritual needs of the church."],[115.12,"Would it be correct to say that the priesthood is something you are rather than something you do?"],[122.48,"Yes. Like St."],[123.68,"Paul's an apostle."],[126.29,"That's not a job."],[128.31,"That's that's who he is."],[130.15,"That's an identity he's received."],[131.99,"Like being a king."],[132.87,"From Christ. And so he's he's an apostle."],[137.37,"And so he would have the right, in order for him to be, for the same reason that he gave up having a wife and children, so he could devote himself to this ministry."],[147.83,"That's the purpose."],[148.95,"The purpose is we'll support you so that you can devote yourself to this ministry, and you're not going to have to go and work a job to support yourself, which will take time and consideration and concern and everything away from what you're able to do."],[164.07,"So it's a question of value."],[168.47,"Of do you value how how a person values the church, the services of the church, the sacraments, values the ministry that the priest is doing."],[180.25,"That they're willing to support him so that he's able to do it."],[185.05,"Um in general, if you go to a church that isn't willing to do that, it's because they don't value the church and what the clergy is doing and what it has to offer."],[198.13,"Because that's how we show that we value things."],[200.53,"We put our money there, regardless of our money and our time, regardless of what we may claim when asked, that's how we show that we actually value something."],[217.49,"But endure all things lest we hinder the gospel of Christ."],[222.42,"So he says, even though, as I've just argued, perfectly alright, probably what you should be doing."],[230.76,"Well because we don't want people to say, oh, that St"],[232.0,"But we haven't come and asked you for this."],[236.0,"We have demanded this."],[237.2,"Why? Paul, he's in it for the money."],[243.1,"Here he comes around again asking for a handout."],[246.44,"And that might hurt his ministry and cause people not to accept what he's preaching if they thought he was doing it for wrong motives."],[256.52,"So he says, so look, I'll give that up."],[258.04,"I'll go work."],[259.08,"He went and worked with his hands making tents."],[261.96,"Not fun, I'm sure."],[264.09,"So, but uh but he did that for the sake again."],[270.13,"So he's again, he's using himself as an example, the point he was making before."],[274.1,"That this is what it means to be a Christian."],[275.86,"Paul has tried to be an example of this to them"],[276.54,"And I've tried to be, and St."],[281.02,"That you're willing to give up these rights and privileges for the sake of others."],[285.42,"He says, I've given them up for your sake and helping plant this church in Corinth."],[290.62,"You need to be willing to give them up for one another."],[305.26,"Even so the Lord has commanded that those who preach the gospel should live from the gospel."],[311.98,"Is he talking about pagan priests or Jewish priests?"],[316.7,"He's talking about Jewish priests."],[317.9,"He's going back to the Torah again."],[320.16,"Because, and what he's pointing to is that uh when you get to And this is usually where people sort of nod off as they you know they start."],[331.4,"I'm gonna read through the whole Bible."],[333.0,"You start out in Genesis, and uh if you make it through Leviticus, somewhere in Numbers."],[340.02,"You sort of drift off."],[341.14,"Well, this is in Numbers."],[342.42,"Numbers is where it has all of the inheritances of each tribe."],[346.34,"Uh huh. All the parcels of land, which is one of the reasons why people drift drift off."],[351.64,"And how many people were in each tribe?"],[354.2,"Um the Levites, from whom all the priests came, Levites had no inheritance."],[361.72,"They got no land."],[363.04,"They got no property."],[364.2,"Or they were supposed to be permanent"],[364.48,"Remember, the land endowments were permanent."],[369.78,"Every 50 years of the Jubilee year is all supposed to revert back."],[374.34,"So this was their inherit permanent inheritance."],[376.98,"Levites had none."],[378.9,"Explicitly says that their inheritance was the Lord."],[383.22,"But, well, how did they eat then?"],[385.3,"They didn't eat by growing crops because they didn't have land to grow crops on."],[390.02,"They didn't have herds, they didn't have any of this stuff."],[392.74,"They lived by, there was a portion of all the offerings that were brought to the tabernacle and then the temple that went to them."],[403.74,"You brought a sacrifice, it was cut up, a portion was offered to God, portion was eaten by the priest, portion was eaten by the people who brought the sacrifice."],[413.26,"That's how it worked."],[415.1,"So St. Paul's pointing to another example in the Torah."],[417.59,"This is the way God set it up."],[419.73,"So he's saying this is, again, this still applies in the church."],[424.21,"We don't have a tabernacle, we don't have just one temple."],[427.49,"But the people who come to preach the gospel of Jesus Christ should be supported in their living for doing that."],[436.63,"Is what he's saying."],[437.35,"It should be the same kind of idea."],[440.39,"They shouldn't be owning palatial estates."],[443.75,"And look, you know, that that's not."],[448.15,"They shouldn't be accumulating wealth."],[449.83,"They shouldn't have business interests."],[451.19,"They shouldn't have."],[451.93,"They should. be how they make their living"],[454.21,"But they have for them to do that, the church then has to support them in doing that."],[464.69,"Verse 15, but I have used none of these things, nor have I written these things that it should be done so to me."],[471.65,"For it would be better for me to die than that anyone should make my boasting void."],[476.45,"For if I preach the gospel, I have nothing to boast of."],[481.73,"Yes, he's referring back this whole uh boasting thing"],[488.29,"He's referring back to all the way back at the beginning of 1 Corinthians."],[492.61,"One of the first problems he addressed was that there were sort of factions growing up in Corinth."],[498.05,"These factions growing up"],[498.41,"Oh, yeah. Where some were saying, I'm of Paul, and I'm of Apollos, and I'm of Cephas."],[503.99,"And he made this whole point about, that new."],[508.37,"He says, Did Paul die for you?"],[509.81,"Were you baptized in the name of Paul?"],[511.65,"No. So he's kind of coming coming back to that, to that theme."],[521.28,"That he's he himself has nothing to boast or brag about."],[527.06,"He's preaching the gospel because he has to."],[531.78,"That's what he's literally saying."],[532.9,"He's saying, I have to."],[534.42,"Why? Well, because Christ appeared to him, remember, and knocked him off the horse."],[538.67,"It said, Go do it."],[542.07,"So, you know, that's what he says, woe to me if I don't."],[545.25,"So that's this isn't something he's, you know, sort of choosing to do where he can brag about, like, hey, I'm the one, you know."],[554.05,"Is a duty that was laid on him."],[557.81,"For if I do this willingly, I have a reward."],[560.45,"But if against my will I've been entrusted with a stewardship."],[565.03,"So that's it."],[565.83,"I didn't choose."],[566.79,"I didn't say, I'm gonna be an apostle."],[568.47,"I'm gonna go and travel and preach, and that's how I'm gonna make a living."],[572.95,"He's saying, I've been entrusted this."],[575.19,"And a stewardship means I'm gonna be judged at the end of the day for how well I've done this task that I've been assigned."],[582.31,"Yeah Yeah. So he's saying whether willingly or not is irrelevant"],[589.23,"Well, no, he's he's he's saying he's making the point in his case that it's unwilling."],[593.63,"Uh-huh. This was given to him, and he's gonna be judged based on how he did."],[599.41,"And so he's out doing it."],[602.45,"That's why he's saying there's no reward."],[605.39,"If you choose to do it, you might get a reward."],[607.8,"I did this thing I didn't have to do."],[609.56,"So people give me thanks and they give me credit, they say, you know, oh hey, here's a tip."],[615.32,"Thanks for doing this."],[616.44,"All right, he's saying you don't get that when it's something you have to do."],[620.68,"Uh-huh. They nobody comes and thanks you for doing your job."],[625.3,"Um so that's what he's saying"],[626.94,"That's um doing what you're getting paid to do."],[632.14,"He's saying, I've been entrusted with this."],[633.82,"Not only am I not going to get a reward for for just for doing it, I'm gonna be judged based on how I do."],[640.04,"It's a task that's assigned me."],[643.32,"He says, What is my reward then?"],[645.64,"That when I preach the gospel, I may present the gospel of Christ without charge, that I may not abuse my authority in the gospel."],[655.66,"And so he's saying, my reward is that would be that I could do this for free."],[662.16,"That I don't have to charge."],[663.6,"I can come and preach to you and not try and take uh take money for it."],[670.16,"Um you may say, well, that sounds weird"],[674.04,"How wouldn't you take money to preach the gospel?"],[677.16,"Remember, he's talking to a lot of Greeks."],[680.76,"If you wanted to become somebody's disciple, if you wanted to go to the academy in Athens, if you wanted to come under the tutelage of some teacher, some philosopher, you were paying them."],[691.48,"Yeah. Sometimes through the nose."],[695.24,"And following their."],[696.76,"And St. Paul is saying, I don't have to do that."],[702.36,"I don't have to do that."],[703.32,"Everything I've given to you, everything I've taught you, everything I've shown you has all been for free."],[715.32,"And that to charge them would have been to abuse his authority in the gospel."],[721.52,"It would have been abuse to take what Trist Christ had entrusted to him and then try and sell it to other people."],[732.32,"Verse 19 For though I am free from all men, I have made myself a servant to all that I might win the more."],[739.92,"And to the Jews I became as a Jew that I might win Jews."],[743.68,"To those who are under the law as under the law, that I might win those who are under the law."],[748.24,"To those who are without law is without law, not being without law toward God, but under law toward Christ, that I might win those who are without law."],[758.08,"To the weak I became as weak, that I might win the weak."],[761.56,"I have become all things to all men that I might by all means save some."],[766.2,"Now this I do for the gospel's sake, that I may be partaker of it with you."],[773.56,"So St. Paul is, again, this is carrying out his argument."],[782.92,"I'm free. I can do what I want."],[785.73,"I could do what I choose to do."],[787.49,"I have the same rights as anybody else, the same privileges."],[791.25,"He says, but I've given them up over and over again."],[794.21,"And I give them up based on who I'm talking to."],[799.65,"So if I'm preaching the gospel to Jews, what he would go into the synagogues."],[803.59,"He said, I become as a Jew."],[804.63,"Well, that means he's not eating pork"],[804.99,"What does that mean?"],[808.65,"That means he's not doing anything."],[811.1,"Not that he doesn't have the right to."],[814.04,"But he's giving it up so as not to give offense."],[817.32,"So not to give a necessary offense so that he can try to reach them with the gospel, with who Christ is."],[822.13,"When he's with those who are under the law, why is that separate from Jews?"],[827.17,"Well, that would include, for example, we talked about the Godfearers."],[830.77,"Oh, okay. The proselytes, the Jesuits are kind of around the fringe of the synagogue."],[834.49,"Who would be keeping parts, probably, not all of it, or they would have become fully Jews."],[839.29,"For example, they weren't getting circumcised."],[841.05,"But we're probably keeping some of it."],[842.71,"So he says, When I'm with them, I become like them."],[845.93,"So if there's parts of the law they're not following, they're not keeping kosher, then maybe I don't necessarily keep kosher."],[852.54,"If he goes to their house and they serve him food that's not kosher, he doesn't get offended and storm out."],[860.6,"Those who are without law."],[862.06,"Those would be the pagans."],[864.72,"He says, I become as those who are without law."],[867.28,"Now notice he wants to be clear though, saying, I'm not saying I become a pagan."],[871.66,"Like he doesn't go worship their pagan gods."],[873.82,"He doesn't go."],[874.79,"He says, because he says, not being without law toward God, but under law toward Christ."],[880.49,"So he's saying, I'm still under the law."],[885.69,"Yeah, yeah. He's saying, I'm not, I'm not saying go out and fornicate because you're trying to reach fornicators."],[888.65,"Yeah Like that's not I imagine that's been used."],[896.5,"I've tried to run drug addicts, so I need to use drugs."],[899.54,"You know, no, that's not what he's And to the weak I became as weak that I might win the weak."],[907.0,"Going back to his argument in chapter eight."],[909.9,"To the weaker brother who is might be offended or caused to give offense by seeing this, hey, I don't do anything that would do that."],[918.23,"This isn't in the context of him going and committing some sins."],[921.35,"This is in the context of him being willing to give up his rights and privileges and things that would be fine for him to do."],[930.63,"And the goal of all that is that he that. he would become all things dull so by any means could reach some of them"],[940.35,"He can reach some of them."],[946.11,"And just a brief note, just a brief note."],[950.27,"Um this is a brief liturgical note."],[954.83,"Um, one thing that a lot of our Protestant friends have a real problem with uh when they first come to an Orthodox service, and often it's Vespers, is uh when they hear most holy Theotokos save us."],[972.13,"Because the verb save applied to the Theotokos or really anybody else other than Christ bothers them."],[982.65,"I will just point out that here in the New Testament, St."],[987.21,"Paul uses the phrase, I might by all means save some."],[997.69,"Paul's already made it clear, he's not saying he died for them."],[1001.77,"He's not saying they were baptized into the name of St."],[1004.57,"Paul. But he's using the verb save, but it's very clear what he means."],[1010.88,"He's talking about bringing people to Christ."],[1013.44,"He's talking about interceding with them before Christ."],[1016.7,"Bringing them to repentance and salvation."],[1019.18,"So if St."],[1019.82,"Paul can do that, there's no reason that Theotokos can't do that."],[1026.59,"That's an important point because I had trouble with that for a number of years."],[1033.55,"Yeah. But yeah, it's it's because there are certain words, and I don't want to go too far down this rabbit hole, because the example I'm about to give is a huge rabbit hole."],[1049.07,"But there are certain words that and a lot of this is because of the way English translations have sort of hidden things from us that are used much more freely in Scripture than we realize."],[1066.25,"So, for example, English Bibles are very reticent to translate save as save unless it's referring to Jesus."],[1074.71,"Unless it's referring to Christ, we don't use save."],[1077.75,"They'll translate it as help or rescue or some other some other synonym."],[1084.31,"Um and the example I'm going to use is the rabbit material, I'm not totally gonna go down, but is the way the word Elohim is used in Hebrew in the Old Testament, which means God or gods."],[1096.61,"It's used for all kinds of things."],[1098.77,"It's used for the spirits of deceased people, it's used for angels, it's used for demons in a couple of places."],[1106.77,"It really just sort of means a spiritual being."],[1109.43,"But when you read an English Old Testament, if it's talking about God, like the God, it's translated as God."],[1117.99,"In a few places where it's talking about pagan gods, it's translated as gods."],[1122.71,"But most of the other places it's translated as spirits or angels or different ways."],[1131.91,"But a lot of these words are a lot more flexible in the original."],[1137.11,"And you can usually, like in this context, before I stopped and made this note, I don't think anyone here was thinking, like, say, Paul is claiming he could save people apart from Christ."],[1149.67,"Like, nobody even noticeded that he used the word sav, because we all knew exactly what he meant."],[1151.35,"Uh-huh Um so yeah, it's a it's it's the flexibility of the word as opposed to a very sort of precise theological definition that we've got in our head that we sort of import every time we see the word."],[1171.08,"It's like, wait, hold up."],[1174.84,"Sometimes a cigar is just a cigar."],[1177.4,"Uh-huh. And sometimes save just means save."],[1181.4,"You know. And notice there at the end in verse 23 that he says he does this for the gospel's sake, that he might be partaker of it with. you"],[1199.61,"So again, this is while this passage, just to make sure it's not coming off as boasting."],[1207.43,"He's saying, I want to make sure that I find salvation like you have."],[1213.51,"Uh-huh. Corinthians, who are tolerating incest."],[1219.32,"So this shows that that humility of St."],[1223.48,"Paul's approach. And also is another example of how he continues to speak to the people at Corinth despite all these problems we're seeing."],[1236.6,"He never addresses them as sort of a bunch of sinful wretches who better, you know, dangling by a spider web over the fires of hell."],[1244.32,"You know, Allah, Jonathan Edwards."],[1246.18,"You better pray that you're not all smote."],[1249.38,"You know, he we talked about this at the very beginning, where he's talking to them as, you know, you're holy ones, you're like the angels of God."],[1256.18,"Now stop committing incest."],[1258.82,"You know, we go, wait, what?"],[1263.86,"But that's what St."],[1265.3,"Paul, St. Paul always talks to them as who they've been created to be."],[1271.38,"This is who you've been created to be, this is who you are in Christ."],[1276.27,"And so every time he talks to them, he's like, You this is what you need to be."],[1279.87,"This is who you are, this is what you need to grow into."],[1284.79,"And what you're doing right now is not that."],[1287.93,"Well, it's also an example, though, of a person who understands their own."],[1297.45,"I mean, you're talking to a person who held the coats while Stephen was stoned."],[1302.73,"I mean who he understands how much God has forgiven him."],[1310.09,"Just what kind of a sinner he was, how serious a sinner he was."],[1315.61,"And so he can look at these other people and not say, you know, you are certainly damned because he's not."],[1325.5,"He knows it."],[1326.54,"He has a real understanding of his position before God."],[1334.02,"Yeah. His own gratitude."],[1336.02,"Yes. Yeah. Yeah."],[1341.3,"Um. This Christ says he has been forgiven much, loves much."],[1350.1,"Um, and so yeah, St."],[1351.94,"Ball was definitely aware."],[1358.66,"Verse 24. Do you not know that those who run in a race all run?"],[1363.86,"But one receives the prize."],[1366.42,"Run in such a way that you may obtain it."],[1369.38,"And everyone who competes for the prize is temperate in all things."],[1373.42,"Now they do it to obtain a perishable crown, but we for an imperishable crown."],[1379.58,"Therefore I run thus, not with uncertainty."],[1382.7,"Thus I fight, not as one who beats the air, but I discipline my body and bring it into subjection, lest when I have preached to others, I myself should become disqualified."],[1394.38,"So it's not just his past, it is his past, but it's also his awareness that he's capable at any moment of falling into the same things they've fallen into."],[1409.48,"It's not that he is some super spiritual class of being."],[1414.28,"I am a saint."],[1415.0,"There's a less here, yes"],[1416.4,"So there's something more great about."],[1422.24,"And his description here of the Christian life is certainly not the description of having achieved some sort of state."],[1433.46,"Having achieved some sort of static state."],[1443.86,"Well, not the one of the fundamental differences between the way in which Orthodox Christianity sees salvation and the way both Roman Catholicism and Protestantism see salvation."],[1460.78,"Both Roman Catholicism and Protestantism see salvation as achieving a certain state, a state of grace."],[1470.86,"That's a particular Roman Catholic language."],[1474.54,"This state where you're resting in God's grace, where your sins have been forgiven."],[1479.75,"Where you are morally perfect."],[1485.13,"And that this state is required to enter into heaven or to enter into eternal life."],[1493.29,"And so then the disagreements between Roman Catholicism and Protestantism are about how you get there, whether you can lose it once you have it."],[1504.82,"And then, you know, sort of other related issues."],[1508.98,"So in the in the Roman Catholic system, that state of grace is gained and lost over and over and over again."],[1517.78,"Sometimes multiple times in the same day."],[1520.74,"It's a day where you go to confession."],[1524.5,"And there's even a certain amount of variation within it."],[1527.94,"It takes a mortal sin to pull you completely out of it."],[1534.1,"But once you confess that mortal sin, uh you're back in a state of grace."],[1541.62,"But even while in a state of grace, uh you continue to have what's called temporal guilt, sort of the residue of of your venial sins and even confessed mortal sins, that has to be taken care of through penance, through acts penance."],[1557.42,"And so that's why, because you have to be perfect to enter heaven, if you're in a state of grace but not perfect, you go to purgatory."],[1565.66,"And everybody in purgatory eventually gets to heaven."],[1569.82,"Um, if you're out, if you die outside of that state of grace, then that's it, you go to hell, no second chance, no purgatory, no nothing."],[1581.02,"Uh but so this is true of any mortal sin in the Roman Catholic system."],[1585.9,"So if Mother Teresa had decided to skip mass one Sunday, which is a mortal sin, uh, not meeting her Sunday obligation, and been hit by a bus on her way to go confess it she would have gone to hell."],[1601.94,"Wouldn't have mattered how she lived the rest of her life, what she did."],[1607.06,"That's interesting. That's it."],[1608.5,"Because in in my research on the effects of the plague, uh one of the things that I encountered was this emphasis, which was apparently new, at least in the amount it was info emphasized, on the state at the moment of death."],[1626.84,"And there are, you know, there are paintings that illustrate this with the the good angel and the demon fighting over the soul of the dying man and and so on and so forth."],[1639.4,"That the state at that moment was what was crucial"],[1645.6,"And this apparently had not been as strongly emphasized, or maybe not emphasized at all before."],[1652.56,"Yeah. Well, there that was a time when there was a lot of sudden death."],[1655.52,"Yeah. Where you might not be able to make it to confession and make adequate preparations and get last rites before you died."],[1661.54,"Yeah, so they were very, very concerned about that."],[1664.18,"Yeah. And and it crops up all over the place."],[1666.5,"I mean, you even see it in Hamlet."],[1668.59,"There's the scene where Hamlet comes to to kill his stepfather, but then realizes he's praying."],[1675.55,"And he says, Well, I don't want to kill him while he's praying, because then surely he would go to heaven."],[1680.99,"I'm gonna wait until he's committing some egregious sin and murder him then."],[1683.39,"So there's that kind of idea there"],[1685.03,"That way he'll go to hell."],[1686.55,"Uh-huh. But so there's this state of grace."],[1692.63,"So reacting against that."],[1694.97,"Martin Luther is reacting against that."],[1696.89,"Martin Luther before the Reformation uh went to confession literally multiple times a day until his confessor in the monastery told him to go away and stop it."],[1706.3,"Like he was just constantly because he really took this to heart."],[1710.64,"And wanted to try and maintain this constant purity, which meant every thought, stray thought he had, he had wanted to go and confess immediately."],[1720.8,"Uh and so reacting against this, this is where he argues for uh what's called the imputation of Christ's righteousness, which is basically Christ earns that state for you."],[1733.8,"You can't be perfect."],[1735.24,"You're the opposite of perfect, it always will be."],[1738.06,"You're a miserable wretch and a sinner."],[1740.3,"But Christ was perfect, and so there's this exchange that happens."],[1745.11,"Christ gives you his perfection and he takes your sinful wretchedness."],[1750.95,"That's a little like the Catholic uh theology of indulgences."],[1756.55,"Yeah. That Christ's perfection is."],[1759.35,"Yes, but it's put out dribs and drabs."],[1761.59,"Yeah. It's infused into you through the sacraments and through indulgences and through all those things."],[1767.41,"Whereas you get it all at once."],[1769.53,"Uh-huh in the Protestant view of justification."],[1769.85,"Yeah Just boom, this exchange happens and it's done."],[1777.1,"And then generally, although it came to be doubted later, you couldn't undo that."],[1783.82,"Once that was done, it was done."],[1786.24,"And you were uh you were gonna go to heaven when you die."],[1792.4,"Yeah, this causes all kinds of other problems in terms of how you envision the Christian life after that, where it sort of becomes like a waiting room where you just try not to do really bad stuff, not because you'll lose your salvation, but just because you shouldn't."],[1808.96,"You know, and so there's lot of a in trying to explain Luther's in my classes, I have I've said. that the justification for uh moral action after that is gratitude"],[1822.68,"Yeah. Uh yeah you know I I'll I'll pick a student and say uh okay suppose uh suppose I could give you an F and you really earned an F but I gave you an A."],[1836.84,"Would you be grateful?"],[1838.68,"Uh yeah uh would you do things that would please me as a result of your gratitude?"],[1845.0,"Well yeah so that's yeah, that's generally the approach."],[1848.84,"And that worked really well for people coming out of the Roman Catholic system."],[1854.84,"Who had a very existential experience of relief from sort of the spiritual burden right that that had been placed on them."],[1864.8,"They really experienced that."],[1866.08,"Yeah. Um for your average evangelical Protestant growing up in the United States in the twentieth or twenty-first century, not so much."],[1874.8,"I don't know how real that existential experience of gratitude is."],[1878.24,"Uh-huh. You know, with some people, especially people who, like Saint Paul, had particularly sinful past and are sort of aware of it, they have that existential experience."],[1889.76,"But if you were raised in an evangelical Protestant home and were baptized when you were twelve or thirteen, you know, I don't I don't know how much of that existential experience of gratitude you actually have."],[1904.92,"You know, and then how and then that creates these misunderstandings of the Christian life, um, which tend to, I don't want to go too far down this rabbit trail, but tend to, at least in our contemporary world, somehow turn into politics."],[1920.84,"Uh, yeah, everything does."],[1922.52,"Either liberal or conservative, politics, depending on which side of that spectrum you are, that sort of the the because we've taken the idea of good deeds that please God, good works that please God out of the mix."],[1938.96,"It somehow takes on this sort of, in our modern contemporary American culture, it's taken on this sort of political dimension of either liberal or conservative politics, depending on which side of the fence your your church and your upbringing are on."],[1953.92,"But all that is to say, it's very much viewed as this state."],[1960.24,"And so there are certain parts of St."],[1961.92,"Paul that you can read in that way."],[1964.96,"As many of you as have been baptized into Christ have put on Christ."],[1968.02,"Boom. Right there you go."],[1970.5,"And there certainly is a decisive moment of justification."],[1973.54,"St. Paul associates it with baptism."],[1975.38,"We'll get into that more later in his epistles."],[1977.94,"There is certainly a moment at which someone becomes a Christian."],[1983.07,"There is a Moa transferation, it's generally associated by St."],[1986.51,"Paul and by the church throughout history, even among the Protestant reformers, uh, with baptism."],[1994.55,"Not so much in contemporary Protestantism."],[1998.63,"So there is a decisive moment."],[2001.11,"There are sort of decisive moments in our Christian life."],[2005.19,"And certainly, if someone is not a Christian at all and becomes a Christian, that is a major."],[2011.37,"If someone converts from Islam or Hinduism or to Christianity, this is obviously a major decisive moment."],[2018.57,"But by and large, that is not about how St."],[2020.89,"Paul talks about the Christian life, and that's not how he's talking about it here in 1 Corinthians chapter 9."],[2027.53,"Here he's talking about running a race."],[2029.53,"This is a distance race."],[2032.65,"And the prize comes at the end"],[2037.95,"He says, everybody runs, only one wins the prize."],[2042.73,"So by pointing that out, he's saying, we don't run this race."],[2047.13,"We don't live the Christian life, you know, just to participate."],[2051.55,"They didn't have participation trophies in the ancient world like we do now."],[2056.59,"There were winners and losers in these races he's talking about."],[2059.55,"Sorry about the Olympic games where you win a crown and other local games."],[2064.35,"The people who are there who are running are running to win."],[2067.31,"They're running to receive the prize at the end."],[2070.43,"They're running to be the victors."],[2073.07,"And that was, it sounds like, well, very few people are saved."],[2077.56,"And that's not what he's saying."],[2078.84,"He's talking about how you run."],[2080.72,"He's saying more or less clear as he goes over"],[2084.82,"He says, uh those who run in a race, they all run, but one receives the prize."],[2090.1,"Therefore, run in such a way that you may obtain it."],[2093.62,"Run to run."],[2095.16,"Not for the participation trophy."],[2096.68,"You need to run like you're trying to win."],[2098.52,"Uh-huh. Like you're trying to lay hold of the crown, the trophy that comes at the end."],[2105.52,"And so he's not, and we naturally in our culture think about things in competitive terms."],[2115.17,"That me winning means somebody else losing."],[2117.75,"That's not what he's saying about salvation."],[2119.35,"Like somehow somebody else has to, I have to knock somebody else out of the salvation. pool in order to get my spot"],[2126.35,"But he is saying that when we're living our Christian life, this means exerting effort."],[2132.53,"That it's not trying to be good enough."],[2136.45,"It's not what what's, you know, there there is this sort of bare minimum idea."],[2142.45,"Like, how often do I have to go to church?"],[2145.33,"Give me a rule."],[2146.47,"I mentioned Sunday obligation in the Roman Catholic system."],[2150.17,"Well, you you know, you could go to Mass every day, but you just you have to go on Sunday."],[2154.57,"And there's even specific rules."],[2156.02,"It has to be after a certain time on Saturday counts and up to a certain time on Sunday counts."],[2163.08,"It's gotta be in that window."],[2165.94,"It''s gs gotta beotta be in in that, it that window."],[2166.5,"that So's requirement one."],[2166.66,"And so we're gonna give you the checklist"],[2168.48,"And then you gotta do that."],[2172.16,"And if you check all the boxes of the checklist, then you've done what's required."],[2176.06,"And you get that's again not how St."],[2180.22,"Paul is talking about it."],[2182.15,"That we're pushing and we're striving."],[2185.16,"And uh he says, I I don't I don't fight like somebody who's shadow boxing in a corner, for exercise."],[2194.22,"I fight like somebody who's in a fight."],[2196.88,"That's a big difference."],[2198.86,"Punching a punching bag as a workout is very different than being in a fight where you're worried you're gonna get hurt, killed, beaten."],[2206.31,"So you're fighting."],[2207.77,"That's the that's what St."],[2210.09,"Paul is talking about."],[2211.13,"And he continues to do this because he doesn't want to, you know, having helped all of you, I don't want to end up dropping out at the end."],[2222.75,"So this is something that continues throughout our life."],[2225.95,"Following after Christ, pursuing Christ."],[2229.25,"Repent. We don't repent, you know."],[2231.89,"Well, I repented of all the really bad stuff."],[2234.71,"All the really gross, so now I'm done."],[2237.03,"Uh-huh. If you encounter actual holy people, of which I am not one, but if you encounter actual holy people, a lot of them are in monasteries, but some of them are not."],[2248.24,"Uh when you encounter one of them. they are struggling more intensely with their sin, not less"],[2257.67,"It's not that they've now gotten to this spiritual height where they're almost perfect and they've achieved some state."],[2263.67,"Quite the opposite."],[2264.55,"They've become more and more aware of the depths of their sinfulness and are fighting harder than I am."],[2272.73,"That's why they're holier, is because they're struggling more."],[2276.89,"They're fighting harder."],[2279.21,"Not because they've arrived."],[2281.21,"And that's a very different view of the Christian life than the view of now I am a Christian, I have done what is required, I'm there."],[2293.93,"So now I'm gonna go to heaven when I die"],[2299.33,"Um it's very different, it's very different."],[2304.53,"Um as I said, different different groups in the West will disagree about what you have to do to get into that state, what you have to do to meet the bar, more or less, some of them a whole lot, some of them relatively little."],[2322.45,"But uh Orthodox Christianity has always said there is no bar."],[2329.05,"Th isere no point at which you've arrived."],[2332.75,"That we continue to press on, and even even uh in St."],[2338.19,"Maximus. the Confessor in particular, this is very important in his writings"],[2342.63,"Even in the world to come, our life of the world to come, we're going to continue to grow and learn and understand."],[2351.35,"That's one of the best things that I found in biological idea."],[2356.23,"Yeah. Even that is not gonna be the static state where we sit on sit around on clouds with harps and luxuriate or sing in a choir 24-7 or whatever other image that people might have."],[2371.43,"Um of what that's of what that's like."],[2376.39,"Um yeah, this is this is this is a major thing."],[2379.11,"ah, this is this is this is a major thing. Ye that's a it a"],[2388.08,"And so what what we're doing in this life, living our lives as Christians continuously, in church, out of church, all the things that we're doing, is setting a trajectory."],[2400.69,"Because this is the this is the other piece of that."],[2403.09,"Well, if there's not a bar, well, how would someone not right um and uh it's not that, there's a bar somewhere, and some people try but just don't make it."],[2419.38,"That's not some someone who, at least hyp the the at least hypothetical person who would experience eternal condemnation, at the last judgment."],[2429.19,"That would not be because they tried real hard and just weren't quite good enough."],[2433.91,"You know, if they just repented of one more sin."],[2436.65,"Or if they'd just gone to church that one Sunday instead of skipping."],[2439.53,"And been hit by the bus."],[2440.73,"They had been hit by the bus that day, if it had been the day before."],[2443.91,"That's that's not that's not how it happens."],[2446.87,"It's it's a trajectory."],[2449.03,"There's an absolute continuity between where we're going and who we are in our life and who we are and where we're going and what we experience in the world to come."],[2466.71,"Because we continue to become. and to change"],[2475.23,"And so that's a that's a very different view."],[2480.43,"Different people have come up with different visual aids for that."],[2484.19,"I've seen like a spiral going up and a spiral going down."],[2488.27,"And it's a spiral, not a straight line, because of course we always fall and get up and fall and get up and fall and get up."],[2496.11,"But that's it."],[2499.47,"And what St."],[2500.67,"Paul is always arguing in terms of proclaiming the gospel of Christ is that it's never too late to reverse course and get back up."],[2512.19,"Because as you said, he was definitely going the wrong direction."],[2517.45,"And going there fast and with zeal."],[2521.78,"You know. He was he was, you know, the ISIS member of his time."],[2527.54,"Uh-huh. He was out there killing the apostates and the the uh infidels."],[2531.95,"The apostles. Yeah."],[2533.63,"But from his perspective, he was out there killing the infidels for God."],[2537.37,"Um that's literally what he was doing."],[2539.85,"Uh so um, and it was not too late for him to turn around and start struggling in the other direction."],[2550.57,"Uh, and so that's why he's willing to give up all his rights and all these things to try and get these other people to turn back. while there's still time"],[2562.85,"And the trajectory idea also makes sense of his telling the Corinthians that you are angels while they're still condoning."],[2575.89,"Yeah. Because that's the trajectory."],[2579.01,"Yeah. That's who they were created to be, and that's where they're headed."],[2583.57,"And because that's who they're created to be, and that's where they're headed."],[2586.29,"That's why they need to give up this other garbage."],[2588.69,"Yeah. That's why we have to be done with worshiping these false gods, worshiping demons."],[2594.13,"That's why we have to be done with sexual immorality."],[2596.45,"That's why we have to be done with these things."],[2598.21,"Because we're going here."],[2599.75,"And that's what that's what he's getting at when he says everyone who competes for the prize is temperate in all things."],[2607.72,"You have to discipline yourself."],[2611.18,"When you're gonna run a race, where you're gonna compete, when you're preparing for a fight, you're preparing for a battle."],[2617.58,"You need discipline, you need preparation."],[2621.27,"All those things are uh are necessary."],[2626.47,"Uh so yeah, that's that's uh that's where he's aingim here."],[2633.03,"That's how it relates to the idea of giving up, giving up privileges and giving up rights."],[2641.03,"You gotta give up, you gotta toss aside every remember."],[2643.79,"They competed, they competed in all these events naked."],[2645.83,"Because you're not gonna be carrying a bunch of stuff"],[2647.15,"Yeah. That's gonna hold you back."],[2653.09,"And so that's another piece of the imagery."],[2655.31,"We need to."],[2656.75,"All this garbage needs to go so that we can run."],[2660.03,"So this is probably a good place to stop."],[2661.87,"Okay, so. Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God."],[2670.27,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."],[2675.63,"That's wholecounsel at ancientfaith dot com"]],"o":[{"t":21.28,"lv":2,"h":"1 Corinthians 9:10-12 - For our sakes","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"<b>For our sakes</b>... he who ploughs should plough in hope\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"If we have sown <b>spiritual things</b>, is it a great thing if we reap your <b>material things</b>?\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"If others are partakers of this right over you, are we not <b>even more</b>?\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Applies the principle <b>specifically</b> - sounds like he's telling them to start giving</li><li class=\"d1\">he <b>is coming for a visit</b>, but that's <b>not his point</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"If anybody asks <b>why priests get paid</b> by churches, this is why\"</li></ul>"},{"t":68.6,"lv":3,"h":"Priesthood is not a job","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Important for understanding the <b>priesthood</b>: being a priest is <b>not a job</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Paul's examples are <b>farming</b> - not employer and wages</li><li class=\"d0\">Priests <b>don't have a job</b>: church supports them so they can <b>devote time to spiritual needs</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Q: priesthood something you <b>are</b> rather than something you <b>do</b>? - <b>Yes</b></li><li class=\"d1\">like Paul an <b>apostle</b>: <b>identity</b> received from Christ, \"like being a king\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Same reason he gave up wife and children: <b>devote himself to ministry</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"we'll support you so you <b>don't have to work a job</b>\" - which takes time, consideration, concern away</li></ul>"},{"t":164.07,"lv":3,"h":"Support as a question of value","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Question of <b>how a person values</b> the church, services, sacraments, ministry</li><li class=\"d0\">Willing to support him so he's <b>able to do it</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Church unwilling → because they <b>don't value</b> what the clergy and church offer</li><li class=\"d0\">\"That's how we <b>show we value things</b>\" - <b>money and time</b>, regardless of what we claim when asked</li></ul>"},{"t":217.49,"lv":2,"h":"9:12 - Endure all things lest we hinder the gospel","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"But endure all things lest we <b>hinder the gospel of Christ</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Though what he argued is <b>perfectly alright</b>, \"probably what you should be doing\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>we haven't come and asked you</b> for this, haven't demanded it</li><li class=\"d0\">Why? Not to let people say: \"Paul, <b>he's in it for the money</b>... comes around again asking for a handout\"</li><li class=\"d1\">would <b>hurt his ministry</b>, people reject his preaching if <b>wrong motives</b></li><li class=\"d0\">So: \"I'll give that up. I'll go work\" - <b>made tents</b> with his hands, \"not fun\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Again uses himself as <b>example</b> = what it means to be a Christian</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>give up rights and privileges for the sake of others</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"I've given them up for your sake and helping <b>plant this church</b>\" → you give them up <b>for one another</b></li></ul>"},{"t":305.26,"lv":2,"h":"9:13-14 - Those who preach live from the gospel","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"The Lord has commanded that those who preach the gospel should <b>live from the gospel</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Q: pagan or Jewish priests? - <b>Jewish priests</b>, back to the <b>Torah</b> again</li><li class=\"d1\">where people nod off: <b>Numbers</b>, inheritances of each tribe, parcels of land</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Levites</b> (all priests came from them): <b>no inheritance</b>, no land, no property</li><li class=\"d1\">land endowments <b>permanent</b> - <b>Jubilee</b> every 50 years, reverts</li><li class=\"d1\">explicitly: their inheritance was <b>the Lord</b></li><li class=\"d0\">How did they eat? No crops, no herds → <b>portion of all offerings</b> to tabernacle and temple</li><li class=\"d1\">sacrifice cut up: portion to <b>God</b>, portion to the <b>priest</b>, portion to the <b>people who brought it</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paul: God set it up; <b>still applies in the church</b></li><li class=\"d1\">no tabernacle, no one temple - but preachers of the gospel <b>supported in their living</b></li></ul>"},{"t":436.63,"lv":3,"h":"Not palatial estates","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Same kind of idea - <b>not</b> owning palatial estates</li><li class=\"d1\">not accumulating <b>wealth</b>, no <b>business interests</b></li><li class=\"d1\">support is <b>how they make their living</b></li><li class=\"d0\">For that, <b>the church has to support them</b></li></ul>"},{"t":464.69,"lv":2,"h":"9:15-18 - Nothing to boast of: a stewardship","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"I have used <b>none of these things</b>\"; \"better for me to <b>die</b> than that anyone make my <b>boasting void</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Refers back to the <b>boasting</b> and <b>factions</b> at the start of 1 Cor</li><li class=\"d1\">\"<b>I am of Paul</b>, Apollos, Cephas\" - \"Did Paul die for you? Baptized in his name?\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"If I preach the gospel, I have <b>nothing to boast of</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">preaches because <b>he has to</b>: Christ appeared, <b>knocked him off the horse</b>: \"go do it\" - \"<b>woe to me</b> if I don't\"</li><li class=\"d1\">a <b>duty laid on him</b>, nothing to brag about</li><li class=\"d0\">\"If willingly, a <b>reward</b>; if against my will, entrusted with a <b>stewardship</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">didn't choose: \"I'm gonna be an apostle... how I make a living\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>stewardship</b> = <b>judged at the end</b> for how well he did the task</li><li class=\"d0\">Q: whether willing or not irrelevant? - in <b>his case</b> it's <b>unwilling</b>; given to him, judged on how he did</li><li class=\"d0\">Reward only if you <b>chose</b> it (\"here's a tip\"); nobody <b>thanks you for doing your job</b></li></ul>"},{"t":643.32,"lv":3,"h":"9:18 - Preaching without charge","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"My reward... present the gospel <b>without charge</b>, not <b>abuse my authority</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Reward = he can do it <b>for free</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Sounds weird - but talking to <b>Greeks</b></li><li class=\"d1\">disciple of a <b>philosopher</b>, the <b>academy in Athens</b> → you <b>paid</b>, \"sometimes through the nose\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul: all taught and shown was <b>free</b></li><li class=\"d0\">To charge = <b>abuse</b>: taking what <b>Christ entrusted</b> and <b>selling it</b></li></ul>"},{"t":732.32,"lv":2,"h":"9:19-23 - All things to all men","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Free from all men... made myself a <b>servant to all</b> that I might <b>win the more</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Jews: as a <b>Jew</b>; under the law: as <b>under the law</b>; without law: as <b>without law</b>; weak: as <b>weak</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"<b>All things to all men</b> that I might by all means <b>save some</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Carries out his argument: \"I'm free... same rights... but <b>given them up over and over</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>based on who</b> he's talking to</li></ul>"},{"t":799.65,"lv":3,"h":"Jews, those under the law, those without law","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Jews</b>: into the <b>synagogues</b>, \"as a Jew\" → not eating <b>pork</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not that he lacks the right - gives it up to avoid <b>unnecessary offense</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Q: <b>under the law</b> separate from Jews? - <b>God-fearers</b>, <b>proselytes</b> around the fringe of the synagogue</li><li class=\"d1\">keeping <b>parts</b> of the law, not circumcised</li><li class=\"d1\">so with them: if they're not keeping <b>kosher</b>, he needn't; served non-kosher food - <b>doesn't storm out</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Without law</b> = the <b>pagans</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not become a pagan: doesn't <b>worship their gods</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"<b>not without law toward God</b>, but <b>under law toward Christ</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">not \"go fornicate to reach fornicators\" or \"use drugs to reach <b>drug addicts</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Weak</b>: back to <b>ch. 8</b> - giving up <b>rights and privileges</b> that would be fine, not committing sins</li><li class=\"d0\">Goal: <b>reach some of them</b></li></ul>"},{"t":950.27,"lv":2,"h":"Liturgical note: \"save us\"","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Protestants struggle at Vespers: \"<b>Most holy Theotokos, save us</b>\" - <b>save</b> bothers them applied to anyone but Christ</li><li class=\"d0\">Here Paul: \"that I might by all means <b>save some</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">not saying he <b>died for them</b> or they were <b>baptized in his name</b></li><li class=\"d1\">clearly means <b>bringing people to Christ</b>, <b>interceding</b>, bringing to <b>repentance and salvation</b></li><li class=\"d0\">→ if Paul can, \"no reason the <b>Theotokos</b> can't\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1033.55,"lv":3,"h":"Flexible words: save, <i>Elohim</i>","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">English translations <b>hide</b> how freely some words are used</li><li class=\"d0\">English Bibles reticent to translate <b>save</b> as save unless it refers to <b>Christ</b> → \"<b>help</b>\" or \"<b>rescue</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><i>Elohim</i> in Hebrew OT = God or gods; used for <b>spirits of the deceased</b>, <b>angels</b>, <b>demons</b></li><li class=\"d1\">really means a <b>spiritual being</b></li><li class=\"d1\">translated God for <b>the God</b>, gods for pagan gods, else <b>spirits</b> or <b>angels</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Nobody here thought Paul claimed to save <b>apart from Christ</b> - all knew what he meant</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Flexibility of the word</b> vs precise theological definition we import</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Sometimes a cigar is just a cigar. And sometimes <b>save just means save</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1181.4,"lv":2,"h":"9:23 - Humility, and how Paul addresses the Corinthians","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"For the gospel's sake, that I may be <b>partaker of it with you</b>\" - not boasting</li><li class=\"d1\">\"I want to make sure I find <b>salvation like you</b>\" - even to Corinthians <b>tolerating incest</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Never addresses them as <b>sinful wretches</b> \"dangling by a spider web over the fires of hell... <b>Jonathan Edwards</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">at the start: \"you're <b>holy ones</b>, like the <b>angels of God</b>. Now stop committing incest\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Always speaks to them as <b>who they've been created to be</b>, who they are in Christ → grow into it</li><li class=\"d1\">what you're doing now is <b>not that</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Comment: Paul <b>held the coats while Stephen was stoned</b> - knows <b>how much God forgave</b></li><li class=\"d1\">so doesn't say \"you are certainly damned\"; <b>gratitude</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Christ: <b>forgiven much, loves much</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1358.66,"lv":2,"h":"9:24-27 - Run to win","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"All run, but <b>one receives the prize</b>. Run in such a way that you may <b>obtain</b> it\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Everyone who competes is <b>temperate in all things</b>\" - <b>perishable crown</b> vs <b>imperishable crown</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"I run thus, not with <b>uncertainty</b>... not as one who <b>beats the air</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"I <b>discipline my body</b>... lest when I have preached to others, I myself should become <b>disqualified</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Not just his past - his <b>awareness he's capable at any moment</b> of falling into the same things</li><li class=\"d1\">not a \"<b>super spiritual class of being</b>\"; not \"I am a saint\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Description of Christian life is <b>not having achieved a static state</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1443.86,"lv":2,"h":"Excursus: salvation as a \"state\" - Rome","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Fundamental difference</b>: Orthodox vs <b>Roman Catholic and Protestant</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Both see salvation as <b>achieving a state</b> - \"<b>state of grace</b>\" (RC language)</li><li class=\"d1\">resting in grace, sins forgiven, <b>morally perfect</b>; <b>required</b> to enter heaven</li><li class=\"d1\">disagree on <b>how you get there</b> and whether you can <b>lose it</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Roman Catholic</b>: state gained and lost <b>over and over</b>, even same day → confession</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>mortal sin</b> pulls you out; confess → back in</li><li class=\"d1\">still <b>temporal guilt</b>, residue of venial and confessed mortal sins → <b>penance</b></li><li class=\"d1\">must be perfect to enter heaven → <b>purgatory</b>; everyone there <b>eventually reaches heaven</b></li><li class=\"d1\">die outside state of grace: <b>hell</b>, no second chance</li><li class=\"d0\">Example: <b>Mother Teresa</b> skips Mass one Sunday, hit by a bus on the way to confession → <b>hell</b>, however she lived</li></ul>"},{"t":1608.5,"lv":3,"h":"Aside: the state at the moment of death","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: in research on the <b>plague</b>: emphasis on <b>state at the moment of death</b>, apparently new</li><li class=\"d1\">paintings of <b>good angel and demon</b> fighting over the dying man's soul</li><li class=\"d0\">Answer: time of <b>sudden death</b> - couldn't reach confession, <b>last rites</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Even in <b>Hamlet</b>: won't kill Claudius <b>while praying</b> - wants him in <b>egregious sin</b> → hell</li></ul>"},{"t":1686.55,"lv":2,"h":"Luther's reaction: imputation","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Luther</b> reacting: went to confession <b>multiple times a day</b> until his confessor told him to stop</li><li class=\"d1\">wanted constant purity, every <b>stray thought</b> confessed</li><li class=\"d0\">Argues <b>imputation of Christ's righteousness</b>: Christ <b>earns that state for you</b></li><li class=\"d1\">you can't be perfect - \"<b>miserable wretch and a sinner</b>\"; Christ <b>exchange</b>: gives perfection, takes your wretchedness</li><li class=\"d0\">Comment: like Catholic <b>indulgences</b> - but infused in <b>dribs and drabs</b> through <b>sacraments</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Protestant: <b>all at once</b>, \"boom... done\"; generally couldn't undo (doubted later)</li><li class=\"d0\">→ <b>going to heaven</b> when you die</li></ul>"},{"t":1792.4,"lv":2,"h":"After justification: gratitude","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Christian life becomes a <b>waiting room</b>: avoid really bad stuff, not to keep salvation but \"because you <b>shouldn't</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Justification for moral action = <b>gratitude</b></li><li class=\"d1\">class example: could give an <b>F</b> but gave an <b>A</b> - grateful? → do things to please me</li><li class=\"d0\">Worked for those <b>coming out of Roman Catholicism</b>: <b>existential relief</b> of the burden</li><li class=\"d0\">Not so for the average <b>evangelical</b> in 20th-21st c. US - baptized at 12 or 13: how real is the gratitude?</li><li class=\"d1\">some, like <b>Paul</b>, with a sinful past, do have it</li><li class=\"d0\">Misunderstandings of the Christian life → in contemporary world turn into <b>politics</b>, liberal or conservative</li><li class=\"d1\">good works that please God taken out of the mix</li></ul>"},{"t":1953.92,"lv":3,"h":"A decisive moment, but not Paul's focus here","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Some Paul readable as state: \"as many as have been baptized into Christ have <b>put on Christ</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Decisive moment of justification</b> - Paul associates with <b>baptism</b>, later in epistles</li><li class=\"d1\">by Paul, the church, even <b>Protestant reformers</b> - <b>not contemporary Protestantism</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Decisive moments in Christian life; convert from <b>Islam or Hinduism</b> = major</li><li class=\"d0\">But <b>not how Paul talks about the Christian life</b>, nor here in <b>1 Cor 9</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2027.53,"lv":2,"h":"The race: no participation trophies","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Here: a <b>distance race</b>, prize at the end</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Everybody runs, <b>only one wins</b>\" → we don't live the Christian life <b>just to participate</b></li><li class=\"d1\">no <b>participation trophies</b> in the ancient world - <b>Olympic</b> and local games, crown for winners</li><li class=\"d1\">runners run <b>to win</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Not \"very few are saved\" - he's talking about <b>how you run</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"Run like you're trying to <b>lay hold of the crown</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Not <b>competitive</b> as we think - <b>my winning ≠ somebody else losing</b>, no knocking someone out of the \"salvation pool\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Means <b>exerting effort</b>, not \"trying to be good enough\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2136.45,"lv":2,"h":"No bare minimum, no checklist","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Bare-minimum idea: \"how often do I have to go to church? <b>Give me a rule</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Sunday obligation</b>: Mass on Saturday after a certain time counts, Sunday up to a certain time - \"<b>in that window</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>checklist</b>: check all boxes → done what's required</li><li class=\"d0\">Not how Paul talks: <b>pushing and striving</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"Not <b>shadow boxing</b> in a corner\" - <b>in a fight</b>, worried you'll be hurt, killed, beaten; \"big difference\" from a <b>punching bag</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Doesn't want to <b>drop out at the end</b> after helping everyone else</li><li class=\"d0\">Continues <b>throughout life</b>: following and pursuing Christ</li></ul>"},{"t":2229.25,"lv":3,"h":"Holy people struggle more","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Repent: not \"repented of all the <b>really gross</b> stuff, now I'm done\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Actual holy people</b> (many in <b>monasteries</b>, some not): <b>struggling more intensely with sin, not less</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not \"almost perfect\", not <b>achieved a state</b></li><li class=\"d1\">more aware of the <b>depths of their sinfulness</b>, <b>fighting harder</b></li><li class=\"d1\">holier <b>because</b> they struggle more</li><li class=\"d0\">Very different from \"now I'm a Christian, done what's required, <b>I'm there</b>\" → heaven when I die</li></ul>"},{"t":2304.53,"lv":2,"h":"Orthodoxy: no bar, no arrival","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">West disagrees on <b>what you must do to meet the bar</b> - some a lot, some little</li><li class=\"d0\">Orthodox: \"<b>there is no bar</b>\" - no point at which you've <b>arrived</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>St. Maximus the Confessor</b>: even in the <b>world to come</b> we continue to <b>grow and learn and understand</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not a <b>static state</b> - no \"sitting on clouds with harps\" or choir 24-7</li></ul>"},{"t":2388.08,"lv":2,"h":"Life as trajectory","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">This life = <b>setting a trajectory</b></li><li class=\"d0\">If no bar, how would anyone fall short? - not <b>\"tried hard but just wasn't good enough\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">the hypothetical person condemned at the <b>last judgment</b> isn't one who missed by <b>one more sin</b> or <b>one Sunday</b> and the <b>bus</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Absolute continuity</b> between who we are now and what we experience in the world to come - we keep <b>becoming and changing</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Visual aids: <b>spiral going up</b> and <b>spiral going down</b></li><li class=\"d1\">a spiral, not a line: \"fall and get up and fall and get up\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2500.67,"lv":2,"h":"It's never too late to reverse course","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Paul's constant message in proclaiming the gospel: <b>never too late</b> to reverse and get up</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul was going <b>the wrong direction</b>, fast and with <b>zeal</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"the <b>ISIS member</b> of his time\" - killing apostates and infidels <b>for God</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not too late to <b>turn around</b> and struggle the other way</li><li class=\"d0\">So he gives up rights and everything to get others to <b>turn back while there's still time</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2562.85,"lv":2,"h":"Trajectory, angels, and discarding what holds us back","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Trajectory explains why he tells Corinthians \"<b>you are angels</b>\" while they're <b>condoning</b> sin</li><li class=\"d1\">who they were <b>created to be</b> and where they're <b>headed</b></li><li class=\"d0\">→ give up <b>false gods</b>, <b>demons</b>, <b>sexual immorality</b>: \"because we're going here\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Everyone who competes is <b>temperate in all things</b>\" → <b>discipline</b> and <b>preparation</b> for a race, fight, battle</li><li class=\"d0\">Ties to <b>giving up rights and privileges</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Athletes competed <b>naked</b> - not carrying stuff that <b>holds you back</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"all this <b>garbage</b> needs to go so that we can run\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Good place to stop</li></ul>"}]});
